      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended with this fence. We'll solve it later, once we can build a function from a situation like this. First, there's a gap in our derivative list. We know sine and cos, but not tan x. Here's where tan turns up. A lighthouse stands one kilometre out from a long, straight beach. Turn its beam x radians from pointing straight in, and the lit spot lands tan x kilometres along the sand. Here's its graph. It runs between vertical asymptotes, walls the curve never meets, at minus pi over two and pi over two. There, the beam runs parallel to the beach. Watch its tangent. Coming in from the left it's steep. It eases to its flattest at zero, where the slope is one, then steepens again. It never gets flatter than that, and it never falls. So why is the slope never below one? And what is it, exactly?"
    },
